WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.443 --> 00:00:03.628
This is basedb:

2
00:00:03.628 --> 00:00:09.156
the collaborative database where
every table is a real Postgres table.

3
00:00:11.179 --> 00:00:17.576
Everything starts in a project. You can
create an empty base, start from a template,

4
00:00:17.576 --> 00:00:21.079
or describe to the AI
what you want to manage.

5
00:00:21.983 --> 00:00:28.574
Let's open the demo: Lumen Studio, a
small design agency, with its clients,

6
00:00:28.574 --> 00:00:33.320
projects, tasks, invoices,
and its customers' reviews.

7
00:00:34.225 --> 00:00:41.291
Fields filled in by artificial intelligence
are only turned on with your consent.

8
00:00:41.291 --> 00:00:48.357
One click, and the base is ready: tables,
rows, views, dashboard and automations.

9
00:00:49.958 --> 00:00:58.003
Each table works like a spreadsheet. But
every field has a real type: single select,

10
00:00:58.003 --> 00:01:01.452
email, link, currency, date, person.

11
00:01:02.352 --> 00:01:07.415
Tables are linked together. For
each client, the number of projects,

12
00:01:07.415 --> 00:01:13.297
the revenue and the average rating roll
up on their own, through the relations.

13
00:01:14.197 --> 00:01:19.944
Filter, sort, group, color: each
view keeps its own settings.

14
00:01:20.850 --> 00:01:26.098
In projects, the duration is a
formula, written in plain English,

15
00:01:26.098 --> 00:01:28.843
and calculated by Postgres itself.

16
00:01:31.842 --> 00:01:37.119
Each row opens as a record, with
all its fields, rich text included.

17
00:01:38.439 --> 00:01:44.656
The team talks right here: a comment, a
mention, and Camille is notified at once.

18
00:01:46.702 --> 00:01:50.594
Her reply arrives live,
without reloading the page.

19
00:01:51.507 --> 00:01:55.896
The record's history keeps every
change, with the value before.

20
00:01:57.324 --> 00:02:02.642
The same rows can be seen six ways.
Here are the tasks as a kanban board:

21
00:02:02.642 --> 00:02:04.900
drag a card to move it forward.

22
00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:10.385
The calendar shows this month's due dates…

23
00:02:13.817 --> 00:02:18.362
the timeline, tasks over
time and their dependencies…

24
00:02:19.439 --> 00:02:24.794
and on the client side, a gallery of
cards, or a list grouped by industry.

25
00:02:29.556 --> 00:02:33.694
Reviews come in through a
survey. It takes the theme,

26
00:02:33.694 --> 00:02:36.661
the color and the font of your choice.

27
00:02:38.162 --> 00:02:42.755
A question can be asked only when
an answer calls for it: here,

28
00:02:42.755 --> 00:02:44.797
only when the rating is low.

29
00:02:45.707 --> 00:02:50.252
A public link is all it takes:
people answer without an account.

30
00:02:53.726 --> 00:02:57.891
Here is what the client sees:
one question per screen,

31
00:02:57.891 --> 00:03:00.283
with the mouse or the keyboard.

32
00:03:01.195 --> 00:03:07.020
A low rating brings one more question.
And when it's sent, confetti.

33
00:03:09.847 --> 00:03:17.336
The answer instantly becomes a row in the
table. And the AI, here Mistral, reads it:

34
00:03:17.336 --> 00:03:22.151
it finds the sentiment, the
topic, and drafts a reply.

35
00:03:23.056 --> 00:03:27.209
Since the review is negative,
an automation alerts the team.

36
00:03:28.859 --> 00:03:34.463
A survey can also become a quiz.
Under each question, its right answer,

37
00:03:34.463 --> 00:03:36.121
and what it is worth.

38
00:03:40.871 --> 00:03:49.335
Each answer is checked at once, by the
server: the right answers never leave basedb.

39
00:03:51.010 --> 00:03:54.746
A mistake? The right answer
shows up straight away.

40
00:03:58.803 --> 00:04:02.956
At the end, the score fills
its ring, with the answer key.

41
00:04:04.361 --> 00:04:09.716
And it is written to the table: sorted
by score, the grid becomes a leaderboard.

42
00:04:11.359 --> 00:04:17.982
Automations are drawn like a diagram:
a trigger, such as a row that changes,

43
00:04:17.982 --> 00:04:20.946
a set time or a click, then steps.

44
00:04:21.855 --> 00:04:28.451
Update or create a row, notify
someone, call a webhook, post to Slack,

45
00:04:28.451 --> 00:04:29.770
or ask the AI.

46
00:04:30.670 --> 00:04:34.034
Every run is traced, step by step: here,

47
00:04:34.034 --> 00:04:38.324
the task we just completed
got its completion time.

48
00:04:39.955 --> 00:04:44.351
Dashboards bring numbers and
charts together, always up to date,

49
00:04:44.351 --> 00:04:47.374
and computed with each
person's permissions.

50
00:04:48.282 --> 00:04:52.748
Each card is a question, asked
with the mouse or in SQL,

51
00:04:52.748 --> 00:04:57.294
and the dashboard can be
shared, even on another website.

52
00:05:00.090 --> 00:05:07.534
Because under every table, there is a
real Postgres table, with a readable name.

53
00:05:07.534 --> 00:05:11.349
You query it in SQL, right inside basedb.

54
00:05:12.259 --> 00:05:15.955
The query can be saved, for
yourself or for the whole team;

55
00:05:15.955 --> 00:05:18.711
everyone runs it with
their own permissions.

56
00:05:21.633 --> 00:05:26.620
And your BI tools, your scripts
or your agents read the same rows,

57
00:05:26.620 --> 00:05:28.660
with no export and no sync.

58
00:05:30.489 --> 00:05:38.413
The history of the base keeps every change,
wherever it comes from: the interface,

59
00:05:38.413 --> 00:05:44.308
the API, an agent, or direct
SQL. And each one can be undone.

60
00:05:45.217 --> 00:05:50.490
Permissions are granted by group:
on a project, a base, a table,

61
00:05:50.490 --> 00:05:52.714
and down to a single field.

62
00:05:54.351 --> 00:05:56.905
For programs and AI agents,

63
00:05:56.905 --> 00:06:02.582
each base has its own REST API
and MCP server documentation,

64
00:06:02.582 --> 00:06:05.610
generated from your permissions.

65
00:06:06.513 --> 00:06:12.485
And in the interface, the Copilot answers
with proposals: a filter, a query,

66
00:06:12.485 --> 00:06:16.257
a column. Nothing changes
without your approval.

67
00:06:20.635 --> 00:06:25.502
basedb is free software,
under the AGPL license,

68
00:06:25.502 --> 00:06:33.409
and installs with a single Docker command.
Your data stays yours: in Postgres.
